{"id": "mock-1", "title": "Hard Module A", "subtitle": "Modeled on the harder second module (Module 2) of the digital SAT R&W section", "timeLimitSeconds": 1920, "questions": [{"id": "rw-wic-d1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Neuroscientist Elena Vardhan contends that the popular notion of a strict \"critical period\"—a window in early life after which the brain supposedly can no longer rewire itself—is ______. Her experiments show that adult mice deprived of vision in one eye still undergo substantial cortical remapping, though the process unfolds more slowly than it does in juveniles."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "verified", "why": "Reversed: Vardhan's finding that adults still remap undercuts the strict notion rather than confirming it.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "unfounded", "why": "Too strong: because adult remapping 'unfolds more slowly than… in juveniles,' a real developmental difference remains, so the notion is not baseless.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "overstated", "why": "The decisive clue 'more slowly than… in juveniles' shows the critical-period idea holds a kernel of truth but is too absolute, since adults still remap; the strict version overreaches."}, {"text": "premature", "why": "Unsupported: the critical-period idea is long-standing and popular, not a hasty new claim, and the evidence shows it is inaccurate rather than early.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-wic-d2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Among the Toraja people of the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, the funeral rites known as Rambu Solo are observed with elaborate, days-long ceremonies that are sometimes held years after a death, once a family has gathered the buffalo, pigs, and other resources the rituals require."}, "stem": "As used in the text, what does the word \"observed\" most nearly mean?", "choices": [{"text": "Marked", "why": "'Observed with elaborate… ceremonies… held years after a death' means the rites are solemnly kept and commemorated; 'marked' captures performing an occasion with ceremony."}, {"text": "Witnessed", "why": "A common sense of 'observe' (to watch), but the rites are carried out, not merely watched—the ceremonies are the observance, not a spectacle.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Detected", "why": "'Observe' can mean to notice, but nothing in the passage concerns perceiving or discovering the rites.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Stated", "why": "'Observe' can mean to remark, but the Toraja are performing rites, not making a comment.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-wic-d3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "According to the theory of memory reconsolidation, each time a stored memory is retrieved it becomes briefly unstable and must be encoded again before it can be filed away. Far from being ______, then, a long-term memory may be altered—strengthened, weakened, or subtly rewritten—every time it is recalled."}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "ephemeral", "why": "Reversed: 'ephemeral' means short-lived, but the passage describes 'long-term' memories that persist while being modified, not memories that quickly vanish.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "conspicuous", "why": "Unsupported: whether a memory is noticeable is not at issue; the contrast concerns whether it stays fixed.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "fragmentary", "why": "Unsupported: the text addresses whether memories change, not whether they are incomplete.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "immutable", "why": "The 'far from being ______… may be altered… every time' structure requires a word meaning unchangeable; 'immutable' is the precise opposite of being rewritten on each recall."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-wic-d4", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Among the Mande peoples of West Africa, a griot is far more than an entertainer. Trained from childhood, a griot commands an immense repertoire of genealogies, praise songs, and historical narratives, reciting from memory the lineages of families stretching back many centuries."}, "stem": "As used in the text, what does the word \"commands\" most nearly mean?", "choices": [{"text": "Directs", "why": "'Command' can mean to give orders, but one manages people, not a body of memorized songs and genealogies.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Possesses", "why": "'Commands an immense repertoire… reciting from memory' after being 'trained from childhood' signals full mastery; to command a subject is to have thorough command of it."}, {"text": "Overlooks", "why": "'Command' can mean to overlook (as a hill commands a valley), but a repertoire of narratives is not a landscape.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Compels", "why": "'Command' can mean to exact (as one commands respect), but a repertoire of knowledge cannot be compelled.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 1}, {"id": "rw-wic-d5", "domain": "craft", "skill": "words-in-context", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 75, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Learning to read profoundly reshapes the brain. As literacy is acquired, a region of the left visual cortex becomes ______ to letter shapes, responding to them far more sharply than to other visual patterns. Researchers have named this region the \"visual word form area.\""}, "stem": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?", "choices": [{"text": "susceptible", "why": "Wrong connotation: 'susceptible' implies vulnerability to harm or influence, not the heightened, selective responsiveness the passage describes.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "accustomed", "why": "Reversed in effect: becoming 'accustomed' implies habituation, which typically dampens a response, whereas the region responds 'far more sharply.'", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "attuned", "why": "The clue 'responding to them far more sharply than to other visual patterns' describes fine, selective calibration—precisely 'attuned.'"}, {"text": "partial", "why": "Wrong register and sense: 'partial to' means fond of or biased toward, which does not describe a cortical region's response profile.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-tsp-d1", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "To reconstruct the diet of Cretaceous marine reptiles, paleontologist Renata Voss analyzed microscopic scratches on the teeth of dozens of mosasaur specimens, reasoning that hard-shelled prey should leave deeper pits than soft-bodied prey does. Her survey found that pit depth varied widely even among individuals recovered from the same rock formation, a pattern she attributed to differences in diet across the population. [[u]]Voss acknowledges, however, that a tooth's scratches accumulate over an animal's entire lifetime and that a single tooth may therefore record prey eaten years apart.[[/u]] Still, she maintains that the broad range of wear patterns points to a species whose members did not all hunt in the same way."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the function of the underlined portion in the text as a whole?", "choices": [{"text": "It presents the central finding of Voss's survey of mosasaur teeth.", "why": "The central finding—that \"pit depth varied widely\"—is stated in the preceding sentence; the underlined portion, signaled by \"acknowledges, however,\" is a concession, not the finding.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It concludes that tooth-wear analysis cannot reliably reveal what mosasaurs ate.", "why": "This overstates the concession; the closing sentence (\"Still, she maintains\") reaffirms the interpretation, so Voss does not conclude the method is unreliable.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It acknowledges a limitation of Voss's evidence that complicates, but in her view does not defeat, her interpretation.", "why": "The sentence grants a weakness of the method—a single tooth \"may therefore record prey eaten years apart\"—which the following \"Still, she maintains\" sentence sets aside to reassert the conclusion, the classic concede-a-limitation move."}, {"text": "It proposes an alternative method that would more reliably distinguish mosasaurs' diets.", "why": "No alternative method is offered anywhere in the text; the sentence only raises a caveat about the tooth-wear approach already described.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-tsp-d2", "domain": "craft", "skill": "text-structure", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from an 1867 essay on the reading of books.\n\nWe are told on all sides that a reader of taste should keep abreast of the newest volumes as they issue from the press, and that to linger over an old favorite is a species of indolence. I confess I have never found the argument persuasive. The book we have read but once we have, in truth, scarcely read at all; its finer veins lie hidden until a second and a third acquaintance bring them to light. He who hurries from novelty to novelty resembles a traveler who, boasting of the many countries he has crossed, has troubled to learn the language of none."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "It disputes a prevailing view about reading in order to argue for the value of returning to familiar books.", "why": "The passage reports the common view (lingering over an old favorite is \"a species of indolence\"), rejects it (\"I have never found the argument persuasive\"), and defends rereading (\"its finer veins lie hidden until a second and a third acquaintance\")."}, {"text": "It laments that modern readers no longer have the patience to finish the long books of the past.", "why": "The essay concerns rereading versus chasing new titles, not readers' patience for finishing long books, which the text never mentions.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "It advises travelers to learn the language of each country they intend to visit.", "why": "The traveler is an analogy for the hurried reader; treating it as literal advice mistakes an illustrative image for the essay's point.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "It affirms that staying current with newly published works is the mark of a discerning reader.", "why": "This is precisely the view the essayist reports only to reject (\"I have never found the argument persuasive\"), so it reverses the text's stance.", "tag": "reversed"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-ctc-c3", "domain": "craft", "skill": "cross-text", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "paired", "text1": "In a widely discussed experiment, researchers dabbed a colored mark resembling a parasite onto cleaner wrasse and placed each fish before a mirror. The wrasse repeatedly scraped the marked spot against surfaces until it was gone. The researchers concluded that [[u]]the fish recognize their own reflections and therefore possess a rudimentary form of self-awareness[[/u]], an ability long thought to be confined to a few large-brained species.", "text2": "Behavioral scientist Tomas Ek cautions that the mark test favors animals whose everyday behavior already involves removing blemishes from bodies. A cleaner wrasse feeds by picking parasites off other fish; confronted with a parasite-like mark, Ek notes, it might scrape at the spot exactly as it would at prey on a neighboring fish, without drawing any conclusion about whose body the mirror depicts."}, "stem": "Based on the texts, how would Ek most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?", "choices": [{"text": "By insisting that no fish is capable of the kind of self-awareness the mark test is designed to detect.", "why": "Ek questions only whether the test demonstrates self-awareness, not whether fish could ever possess it, so this goes further than his position warrants.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "By granting that the wrasse perceive the reflection as themselves while denying that this perception requires self-awareness.", "why": "Reverses Ek's point: he doubts precisely that the fish takes the reflection to depict its own body.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "By proposing that the wrasse scrape at the mark because they mistake the reflection for a rival fish intruding on their territory.", "why": "Introduces a territorial-aggression motive that Ek never offers; his alternative is ordinary parasite-feeding behavior, not defense against a rival.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "By suggesting that the scraping is fully explained by the fish's habitual parasite removal, so the test does not establish that the wrasse recognizes the reflection as itself.", "why": "Text 2 argues the wrasse 'might scrape at the spot exactly as it would at prey,' meaning the behavior needs no self-recognition to explain, which is what Text 1 infers."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-cid-c1", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Perforated antler batons from Upper Paleolithic Europe have been variously interpreted as spear-straighteners, tent fasteners, and emblems of status. Archaeologist Miriam Kessler's microscopic analysis of forty batons found that wear inside their perforations—smoothed grooves spiraling in a consistent direction—closely matches the wear produced when plant fibers are drawn through replica batons to twist them into rope. Kessler is careful to note, however, that such wear appears on only some specimens and could have accumulated even if rope-making was an occasional, secondary use; her findings thus identify a task the batons could perform without establishing the purpose, if any single purpose existed, for which they were made."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Kessler's analysis establishes that the batons were manufactured as rope-making tools and that competing interpretations of the artifacts should therefore be set aside.", "why": "The text explicitly denies this: the findings identify a task the batons \"could perform\" and the wear \"could have accumulated even if rope-making was an occasional, secondary use,\" so nothing about manufacture is established and rival interpretations are not dismissed.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "The wear inside the batons' perforations consists of smoothed grooves that spiral in a consistent direction.", "why": "This accurately restates a supporting detail about the wear pattern, but that detail is evidence for the larger conclusion, not the main idea of the text.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Kessler's analysis suggests that at least some batons were used in making rope, but it stops short of establishing the primary purpose for which the batons were made.", "why": "The passage moves from the wear evidence matching rope-making to Kessler's explicit caveat that the findings identify \"a task the batons could perform without establishing the purpose . . . for which they were made\"—exactly the two-part idea this choice captures."}, {"text": "Interpretations of prehistoric artifacts should be tested with microscopic wear analysis whenever the artifacts' functions are disputed.", "why": "The text reports one analysis and its limits; it never makes a general methodological recommendation about disputed artifacts as a class.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-cid-c2", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from Edith Wharton's 1911 short story \"Xingu.\"\n\nMrs. Ballinger was one of the ladies who pursue Culture in bands, as though it were dangerous to meet it alone. To this end she had founded the Lunch Club, an association composed of herself and several other indomitable huntresses of erudition. The Lunch Club, after three or four winters of lunching and debate, had acquired such local distinction that the entertainment of distinguished strangers became one of its accepted functions; in recognition of which it duly extended to the celebrated novelist Osric Dane, on the day of her arrival in Hillbridge, an invitation to be present at the next meeting."}, "stem": "Based on the text, which choice best describes the Lunch Club?", "choices": [{"text": "It is ostensibly devoted to the pursuit of learning, but its activities seem driven as much by its members' desire for social distinction as by genuine intellectual interest.", "why": "The narrator's ironic descriptions—pursuing Culture \"in bands,\" members as \"indomitable huntresses of erudition,\" and the club prizing the \"local distinction\" that lets it entertain celebrities—signal that standing, not learning itself, animates the club."}, {"text": "It was founded chiefly to entertain distinguished visitors to Hillbridge but has since turned its attention to lunching and debate.", "why": "This reverses the sequence: entertaining distinguished strangers became an \"accepted function\" only after winters of lunching and debate had earned the club distinction; it was not the founding purpose.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "It has earned a reputation for scholarly rigor that extends well beyond Hillbridge.", "why": "The text says the club's distinction is \"local,\" and the mocking tone never credits the members with rigor, so a far-reaching scholarly reputation inflates what the text supports.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "It was formed because Hillbridge offered its members few other opportunities for intellectual companionship.", "why": "Nothing in the text addresses what other opportunities Hillbridge offered; this motive is invented rather than implied.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-cid-c3", "domain": "information", "skill": "central-ideas", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 85, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Because a proto-language's vocabulary can be partly reconstructed by comparing its descendant languages, historical linguists often treat reconstructed words as evidence of how the proto-language's speakers lived: if terms for \"sow,\" \"grind,\" and a cereal crop can all be traced back to Proto-Palaungic, its speakers presumably farmed. Linguist Dara Chhim cautions, however, that this inference can be subverted by early borrowing. A word adopted from a neighboring language just as a family began to diverge would be passed down within many branches and reshaped by each branch's regular sound changes, thereby acquiring exactly the distribution and form that linguists treat as the signature of inherited vocabulary. Agricultural terms, which tend to travel with the crops they name, may be especially prone to this masquerade."}, "stem": "Which choice best states the main idea of the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Chhim has demonstrated that Proto-Palaungic speakers borrowed their agricultural vocabulary from a neighboring language rather than inheriting it.", "why": "Proto-Palaungic appears only as a hypothetical illustration of the standard inference; Chhim's point is a general caution, and the text reports no demonstration about Palaungic borrowing.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "Comparing descendant languages enables linguists to reconstruct a proto-language's vocabulary but not to identify the regular sound changes that each branch underwent.", "why": "This gets the text backward: the argument presumes that each branch's \"regular sound changes\" are well understood—that is precisely why a reshaped early borrowing can mimic inherited vocabulary.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Terms for crops and farming practices typically spread from one language to another along with the crops themselves.", "why": "The text does note this, but only as a closing detail explaining why agricultural terms are especially vulnerable; it is support for the main argument, not the argument itself.", "tag": "too-specific"}, {"text": "Because words borrowed early in a language family's history can come to resemble inherited vocabulary, reconstructed terms may be an unreliable guide to how a proto-language's speakers lived.", "why": "This synthesizes the whole passage: the opening states the inference from reconstructed vocabulary to speakers' lives, and Chhim's caution shows that early borrowings can acquire \"exactly the distribution and form\" of inherited words, undermining that inference."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-cet-d1", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Many ceratopsian dinosaurs bore an elaborate bony frill extending back from the skull, and the function of these frills has long been disputed. Some paleontologists propose that a frill served as armor shielding the neck from predators; others contend that its broad surface helped the animal shed excess body heat. Naoko Ito and colleagues favor a third explanation, arguing that the frill's primary function was visual display—a structure whose chief purpose was to signal to other members of the same species."}, "stem": "Which finding, if true, would most strongly support Ito and colleagues' hypothesis?", "choices": [{"text": "Many well-preserved frills bear deep punctures whose spacing matches the tooth rows of large predators known from the same deposits.", "why": "This supports the armor-against-predators explanation—the rival hypothesis Ito rejects—rather than the display claim.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "The bone of the frill was permeated by a dense network of channels that once carried blood vessels close to its surface.", "why": "A vascularized surface is consistent with the heat-shedding (thermoregulation) hypothesis, a different explanation, and does not bear on visual display.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "In several ceratopsian species, the frill was proportionally small in juveniles and enlarged dramatically only as individuals approached reproductive maturity.", "why": "Structures that develop specifically at sexual maturity are a hallmark of display or signaling; armor and heat-shedding would be needed at all ages, so this age-linked timing points uniquely to the display mechanism Ito proposes."}, {"text": "Fossil frills of this group rank among the most frequently recovered dinosaur remains at several quarry sites.", "why": "How readily frills fossilize speaks to preservation and sampling, not to what the frill was for, so it cannot support the display claim.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-cet-d2", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-textual", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 90, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "The following text is adapted from a literary historian's discussion of an anonymous 1848 English essay on rural retirement. According to the historian, the essayist presents periodic withdrawals from city life to a country cottage not as a flight from society but as a discipline that returns the writer to social life with sharpened powers of judgment."}, "stem": "Which quotation from the essay would best illustrate the literary historian's claim?", "choices": [{"text": "“I go among the fields not to forget the town but to see it the more truly; a month among the hedgerows, and I come back able to weigh my neighbors' bustle at its just worth, neither dazzled by it nor deceived.”", "why": "This shows the rural retreat (“among the hedgerows”) producing a return to society with keener judgment (“weigh my neighbors' bustle at its just worth”), capturing both halves of the historian's claim."}, {"text": "“Blessed is the cottage that shuts out the clamor of the street, where a man may at last be quit of the whole wearisome commerce of his fellows.”", "why": "This treats retreat as an escape from and rejection of society—precisely the reading the historian says the essayist does not hold.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "“The city rewards the loud and the grasping, and its applause is a coin current only among those who have never known a quieter mint.”", "why": "This criticizes urban society but says nothing about rural retirement sharpening the writer's judgment, so it illustrates only part of the claim.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "“No painter's canvas can rival the slow green fire of an April hedgerow, nor any organ the wind that stirs it.”", "why": "This praises the countryside's beauty but omits any return to society with improved judgment, so it addresses too narrow a piece of the claim.", "tag": "too-specific"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-ceq-c3", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "When animals forage in groups, each individual can rely on its neighbors to help watch for predators, potentially freeing up time to feed—a proposed benefit known as the “many-eyes” effect. To test it, researchers Nadia Okonkwo and colleagues filmed superb fairywrens foraging alone and in groups of two, four, and eight, recording both the percentage of observed time each bird spent scanning for predators and the percentage it spent feeding. The team concluded that a fairywren's investment in watching for predators decreases as the size of its foraging group grows.", "chart": {"type": "groupedBar", "title": "How Superb Fairywrens Spent Their Time, by Group Size", "xLabel": "Group size", "yLabel": "Percentage of observed time (%)", "categories": ["Solitary", "Pairs", "Groups of 4", "Groups of 8"], "series": [{"name": "Time spent scanning", "values": [45, 34, 22, 13]}, {"name": "Time spent feeding", "values": [40, 52, 63, 74]}], "yFormat": "number"}}, "stem": "Which choice best describes data from the graph that support the researchers' conclusion?", "choices": [{"text": "Birds in larger groups spent a smaller percentage of their time scanning for predators than birds in smaller groups did.", "why": "Scanning time falls steadily as group size rises (45, 34, 22, 13), directly supporting the claim that predator-watching investment decreases with group size."}, {"text": "Birds in larger groups spent a larger percentage of their time feeding than birds in smaller groups did.", "why": "This trend is real (feeding rose from 40 to 74), but the conclusion concerns scanning for predators, not feeding, so it does not support that specific claim.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "The percentage of time spent scanning for predators was greatest among birds in the largest groups.", "why": "Scanning was greatest among solitary birds (45 percent) and least in groups of eight (13 percent), so this reverses the actual trend.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "Solitary birds and birds in pairs devoted roughly equal percentages of their time to scanning for predators.", "why": "Solitary birds scanned 45 percent of the time versus 34 percent for pairs—an 11-point gap, not roughly equal.", "tag": "unsupported"}], "answer": 0}, {"id": "rw-ceq-c4", "domain": "information", "skill": "evidence-quant", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "chart", "text": "Employers often resist raising wages, arguing that higher labor costs outweigh any benefits. To test whether higher pay might instead reduce costly employee churn, economist Lena Voss tracked annual turnover rates at fast-food restaurants in a city that raised its minimum wage in 2019 (the treatment group) and at comparable restaurants in a neighboring city that left its minimum wage unchanged (the control group). Comparing turnover in the two cities from 2018 through 2021, Voss concluded that ______", "chart": {"type": "line", "title": "Annual Employee Turnover Rate at Fast-Food Restaurants", "xLabel": "Year", "yLabel": "Annual turnover rate (%)", "categories": ["2018", "2019", "2020", "2021"], "series": [{"name": "Treatment city", "values": [58, 52, 44, 40]}, {"name": "Control city", "values": [57, 56, 55, 54]}], "yFormat": "number", "note": "The treatment city raised its minimum wage in 2019."}}, "stem": "Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the sentence?", "choices": [{"text": "the wage increase caused turnover at the control-city restaurants to rise between 2018 and 2021.", "why": "Control-city turnover fell (57 to 54), not rose, and those restaurants never received the wage increase, so the statement misreads both the trend and the setup.", "tag": "reversed"}, {"text": "from 2019 to 2021, turnover at the control-city restaurants fell by more than 10 percentage points.", "why": "Control-city turnover moved only from 56 to 54—a 2-point drop—far short of 10 points.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "from 2018 to 2021, turnover declined more sharply at the treatment-city restaurants than at the control-city restaurants.", "why": "Treatment-city turnover fell 18 points (58 to 40) while control-city turnover fell only 3 points (57 to 54), the contrast that supports the idea that higher pay curbed churn."}, {"text": "in each of the years shown, turnover was lower at the treatment-city restaurants than at the control-city restaurants.", "why": "In 2018 the treatment city's rate (58) was actually higher than the control city's (57), so this fails in the first year.", "tag": "too-broad"}], "answer": 2}, {"id": "rw-inf-b3", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "Charles W. Chesnutt's \"The Goophered Grapevine\" (1887), the first short story by an African American writer to appear in the Atlantic Monthly, is often read as a sly subversion of \"plantation school\" fiction, in which white authors depicted the antebellum South nostalgically. Chesnutt adopts the school's stock conventions—a genial white narrator, a formerly enslaved storyteller speaking in dialect—only to have the storyteller, Uncle Julius, turn each nostalgic tale to his own material advantage. Literary historian Renata Boyd observes, however, that the magazine did not disclose Chesnutt's racial identity, of which its readers in 1887 were almost certainly unaware. If the story's subversive force depends even partly on readers' knowledge of its author's identity, then ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "Chesnutt concealed his racial identity in order to ensure that the story would be mistaken for conventional plantation fiction.", "why": "The passage attributes the nondisclosure to the magazine, not to Chesnutt, and says nothing about his motives; his intending to be 'mistaken' is invented.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "the story cannot accurately be described as a subversion of plantation-school conventions.", "why": "Overreaches: the conditional says the subversive force depends only 'even partly' on knowledge of the author, and Julius's turning each tale to his own advantage remains in the text no matter who reads it.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "the story's use of a dialect-speaking storyteller links it to other regionalist fiction of the 1880s.", "why": "Plausibly true of 1880s regionalism, but it does not follow from the if-clause about readers' ignorance of Chesnutt's identity and so cannot complete the conditional.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}, {"text": "the story's first readers may well have received it as an example of the very tradition it now seems to undermine.", "why": "If part of the subversion depends on knowing the author was Black, readers 'almost certainly unaware' of that fact could miss it—so the original audience may plausibly have taken the story as the plantation fiction whose stock conventions it adopts. The hedged 'may well' matches the conditional premise."}], "answer": 3}, {"id": "rw-inf-b4", "domain": "information", "skill": "inferences", "difficulty": "hard", "targetSeconds": 95, "passage": {"type": "single", "text": "When goods travel through \"down-the-line\" exchange—passed from one neighboring community to the next—their abundance at archaeological sites typically declines steadily with distance from the source, because each community along the chain keeps a portion before trading the rest onward. Archaeologist Paloma Reyes analyzed obsidian from the Cerro Varal source at dozens of sites in western Mexico and found the expected pattern: the obsidian's share of each site's stone-tool assemblage shrinks with distance. The exception is La Garita, a settlement roughly 300 kilometers from the source, where Cerro Varal obsidian is more plentiful than at many sites within 100 kilometers of it. Reyes therefore suspects that ______"}, "stem": "Which choice most logically completes the text?", "choices": [{"text": "down-the-line exchange played little role in the distribution of Cerro Varal obsidian across western Mexico.", "why": "Too sweeping: Reyes found 'the expected pattern' of steady decline at dozens of sites, so one anomalous settlement cannot indict down-the-line exchange region-wide.", "tag": "too-broad"}, {"text": "La Garita obtained at least some of its obsidian outside the down-the-line network—through direct expeditions to the source, perhaps, or a special exchange relationship.", "why": "Down-the-line exchange predicts steady decline with distance; La Garita's abundance at 300 kilometers violates that prediction while the regional pattern holds, so some more direct acquisition route is the only completion consistent with both the model and the anomaly—hedged with 'at least some' and 'perhaps.'"}, {"text": "the obsidian recovered at La Garita has been mistakenly attributed to the Cerro Varal source.", "why": "The attribution is presented as a finding of Reyes's analysis; the puzzle concerns the quantity at La Garita, and nothing in the passage casts doubt on the sourcing itself.", "tag": "unsupported"}, {"text": "obsidian's usefulness for toolmaking explains why communities in western Mexico traded it across long distances.", "why": "Plausibly true, but it accounts for long-distance trade in general, not for why one distant site holds more obsidian than many nearer ones—the specific anomaly Reyes must explain.", "tag": "true-but-irrelevant"}], "answer": 1}]}
